<p>NASA’s Artemis II crew — three Americans and one Canadian — is more than halfway to the moon and preparing for a historic lunar flyby expected on Monday, the first crewed moon mission in more than 53 years. If all goes as planned, the astronauts will pass into the moon’s sphere of influence and travel farther from Earth than any humans before, while photographing and observing the lunar far side during the flyby. The trip has also brought a familiar but manageable spacecraft headache: the Orion capsule’s toilet is again having trouble flushing, and astronauts have reported an odor from the bathroom. Even so, the mission remains on track, and the crew is continuing scientific observations with a surprisingly low-tech tool — their own eyes — as NASA uses direct human observation of the moon’s surface to support future exploration plans.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Vf1hsVJ2OjETO8ez0M3WlIjR-K5R6FDJJJmXgj09BuuJHIXmUFfJTTfjyXQ4OrtTzOh2Es6giJniq9qMM9f3S34yC6fb8shlP5Lx43TO7faeJUR49cf-O8Hap3phCpEGM6myuCGBVvd2BS5lqgBgk7aJucwhRfZZXTHXUvRNXaQYbZnrKmjyKL-n_PXw6cSpfGoLypK4Jt5AP1eiDj-xc7NJKMlO69otoq_ptU70O5nYU7v0Usm9vZq3a6GMZg' alt='NASA astronaut and Artemis II Commander Reid Wiseman peers out of one of the Orion spacecraft&#x27;s main cabin windows, looking back at Earth, as the crew travels towards the Moon, April 2, 2024.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Record distance: The crew is set to break Apollo 13’s record for the farthest humans have traveled from Earth, according to mission coverage and NASA officials cited in reports.</li><li>Human observation: Astronauts are studying the moon’s surface mainly by looking out the windows, and those observations could help identify features and conditions relevant to later Artemis missions.</li><li>Program significance: Commentary on NPR said Artemis reflects years of planning and is meant to lay the groundwork for sustained human exploration beyond low Earth orbit, not just mark a single flyby milestone.</li><li>Crew makeup: The mission includes one Canadian alongside three NASA astronauts, making it a multinational crew for the first human lunar voyage since the Apollo era.</li></ul><blockquote>The Earth is quite small, and we are so privileged to get this perspective. - Reid Wiseman</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NASA mission management: NASA officials cited in reports have treated the toilet malfunction and odor as an operational problem to manage while keeping the broader lunar flyby plan on schedule. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/halfway-to-the-moon-astronauts-on-artemis-ii-report-of-smelly-toilet-break-apollo-13s-record-nasas-artemis-ii-moon-mission-11312634#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li><li>Artemis astronauts: Crew members have focused on the view back toward Earth and the sense of perspective the journey brings as they continue toward the moon. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/us/science-technology/article/3349061/us-artemis-iis-moon-trip-faces-challenging-toilet-mission-again-over-flushing'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Science coverage: Reporting highlighted that NASA still values what astronauts can learn simply by looking, suggesting human observation remains important even in an era of advanced sensors. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/artemis-astronauts-to-study-the-moons-surface-using-mainly-their-eyes'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Space policy analysts: NPR’s discussion framed Artemis II as proof of how much preparation it takes to restart crewed lunar missions and as a key test for later deep-space goals. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/04/nx-s1-5771727/the-significance-of-the-space-mission-thats-making-history'>NPR</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/us/science-technology/article/3349061/us-artemis-iis-moon-trip-faces-challenging-toilet-mission-again-over-flushing'>US Artemis II’s moon trip faces challenging toilet mission again over flushing</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/halfway-to-the-moon-astronauts-on-artemis-ii-report-of-smelly-toilet-break-apollo-13s-record-nasas-artemis-ii-moon-mission-11312634#publisher=newsstand'>Halfway To The Moon, Artemis II Astronauts Report A Smelly Toilet</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/artemis-astronauts-to-study-the-moons-surface-using-mainly-their-eyes'>Artemis astronauts to study the Moon’s surface using mainly their eyes</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/science/article/2026/04/04/artemis-astronauts-prepare-for-historic-lunar-flyby_6752123_10.html'>Artemis astronauts prepare for historic lunar flyby</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/04/nx-s1-5771727/the-significance-of-the-space-mission-thats-making-history'>The significance of the space mission that&#x27;s making history</a> - npr.org</li></ul>